    "evidence_text": "In Vitro\u2013expanded Antigen-specific Regulatory T Cells Suppress Autoimmune Diabetes\n\nCD28 and ITK signals regulate autoreactive T cell trafficking\n\nThese studies reveal a mechanism of Treg-cell-mediated suppression mediated by miRNA-containing exosomes.\n\nHowever, Treg cells lacking expression of integrin \u03b1v\u03b28 were unable to suppress pathogenic T cell responses during active inflammation.\n\nPrevious data indicates that Tregs may affect the microenvironment redox state.\n\nNcf1-deficient Tregs suppressed Ncf1-deficient Teff very poorly compared to wild type controls.\n\nOxidants may represent a potential new target for therapeutic modulation of Treg function.\n\nThese cells inhibited the responses of CD4+ cells to the mAb itself and to antigen.\n\nIrrelevant regulatory T cells only mediate a partial protection from GVHD.\n\nAdditionally, the use of specific regulatory T cells was compatible with some form of graft-versus-tumor activity.\n\nThis approach may enable future cell-based therapeutic application in inflammatory bowel disease, as well as other inflammatory disorders.\n\nCytokine production by Teffs remained continually suppressed by Tregs.\n\nDepletion of Tregs significantly decreased mortality following cecal ligation and puncture.",
